  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 2 PM, thunderstorms are beginning to initiate in the \nexpected areas in the White and Chirichua Mountains. Activity is\nexpected to increase in coverage and intensity this afternoon \nand evening. The multi-hazard impacts of severe winds and flash \nflooding are still on track. \n\nLooking to tomorrow, there can be another day of multi-hazard\nimpacts of severe wind and flash flooding. For the strong to\nsevere winds, the latest 11/15Z U of A WRF HRRR (usually the \nmost bullish of the models) is showing the potential for a line \nof thunderstorms forming forming from initial activity the \nHuachucha Mountains/Santa Cruz County moving northwest towards\nthe Tucson Metro into Central Pima and Pinal Counties. This is\nthe reasonable worst case scenario, with the more likely\nscenario being thunderstorms being strong to severe but more\nscattered in nature, not in a formal line. The 11/12Z HREF is\nshowing a 10% probability of 50 kts within 40 km in mainly\nCochise County with this run. I wouldn't be surprised if future\nHREF runs spreads this contour westward as it accounts for the \nHRRR's latest runs that are latching onto more activity and \nstronger outflow winds. Moisture from the west and south moves \nback into the eastern valleys, allowing for more of a flash \nflood threat for areas Tucson eastward. The 11/12Z HREF is \nhighlighting these areas for 3 hour probabilities of storm \ntotals between 1-2 inches, with a localized threat for 3 inches.\n\nThe elevated monsoonal pattern continues into Thursday this week\nwith day to day variability. With the high pressure staying in\nthe four corners region, the easterly flow aloft will keep the\nability for the afternoon/early evening activity to move out of\nthe area and allow for the atmosphere to recharge each night.\nThe only caveat to this would be any overnight convection that\ncould hamper and/or shut down the activity for the next day.\n\nOn the bright side, the increased thunderstorm activity will\n'cool' high temperatures, especially Tuesday through Thursday.\nTemperatures begin to warm up Friday into early next week.",
